    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1318">

                <pb facs="1191" n="933" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg933"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 701.6311/152</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Austro-Hungarian Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dumba</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Third
                        Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Phillips</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New York</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-08">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 8, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Phillips</hi>: As the papers begin to
                    publish spurious texts of my letter to Baron Burian, I should like to be able to
                    refer to the real one, or at least to its English translation cabled to the
                    State Department. Could you be kind enough to ask Mr. Lansing to have a copy of
                    it forwarded to me to New York, St. Regis Hotel, still to-day, as I shall only
                    return to Lenox on Saturday from New York.</p>
                <p>These papers are a nightmare.</p>
                <p>Many thanks from yours very sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">C. Dumba</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
